# Per-release checksums: generated at release time and uploaded as a release
# asset, which is where users verify against. Keeping copies in the repo just
# accumulates stale duplicates of what GitHub already serves.
SHA256SUMS-*.txt

# Documentation intermediate build artifacts
release_docs_*/*.json
*.prep.json
*.filled.json
checksums.sha256

# Internal Automation & Build Scripts - NEVER UPLOAD TO GITHUB
#
# NOTE the trailing /* rather than a bare /scripts/. Git cannot re-include a
# file when its PARENT DIRECTORY is excluded, so `/scripts/` plus a `!` negation
# below is silently inert - the negation looks right, changes nothing, and you
# only find out by testing. Excluding the CONTENTS leaves the exceptions able to
# take effect.
/scripts/*
/Documentation.Scripts/
*.py
# GORILLA: exception — the vendored pfind engine is SOURCE, not a local script.
# It is embedded into the binary (internal/llm/tools/find.go) and shipped in
# the .deb; it must be in the repo on every machine that builds it.
!internal/llm/tools/pfind.py

# GORILLA: exception (2026-09-01) — the vendored code-review toolkit is SOURCE
# for exactly the same reason, and this exception was missing.
#
# Commit 042422b vendored the toolkit and wired /review to it, but `*.py` above
# swallowed the engine itself, so code_review.py was never committed. The tree
# has toolkit/configs/ and toolkit/rule_docs/ and no engine — which is why
# TestEveryModuleTheToolkitImportsIsEmbedded fails with
# "open toolkit/code_review.py: file does not exist", and why /review cannot
# work from a fresh clone at all.
#
# The rule is fixed here so the file is committable. The FILE still has to be
# re-vendored: it is not on this machine either, so nothing can restore it from
# the repo.
!internal/llm/tools/codereview/toolkit/**/*.py

# GORILLA: exception (2026-08-20) — the two BENCHMARK scripts are published,
# because the pages that report their numbers tell readers to re-run them.
#
# docs/BENCHMARKS.md has said "the script is in this repo" since July and linked
# to a file that was never tracked: a 404 for every reader, under a heading
# inviting them to check our work. That is the "transparent in theory, closed
# door in practice" failure PHILOSOPHY.md exists to prevent, and it was found
# only when a second page was about to repeat it.
#
# ONLY these two, and only because they are safe to publish: standard library
# only, no credentials (the key comes from the environment), no internal paths,
# nothing about how this machine is laid out. The build, release and packaging
# scripts stay untracked — they are automation for here, not evidence for
# anyone else.
!/scripts/benchmark-models.py
!/scripts/benchmark-tool-discipline.py
!/scripts/measure-network-cost.py
*.sh

# GORILLA: exception (2026-08-20) — build-arch.sh is PUBLISHED. This narrows the
# "packaging scripts stay untracked" line above; that line still holds for
# build-deb.sh, build-tarball.sh and the release tooling.
#
# WHY THIS ONE. The .deb is a finished artifact and a Debian user never needs the
# script that made it. Arch is different: the distro's whole convention is that
# you can read and rebuild the recipe, and the repo already invites exactly that
# — packaging/PKGBUILD documents `makepkg -si` as install route 1.
#
# It also stops a real class of loss. On 2026-08-20 this script was found to be
# missing pfind entirely and to declare one dependency of three, so `pacman -U`
# reported a clean install and the find tool then refused to run. The fix lived
# only on the build machine. An untracked file has no history, no diff and no
# backup: if this machine dies the recipe dies with it, and the .deb-only
# survivors would not reveal what the Arch package was supposed to contain.
#
# SAFE TO PUBLISH, checked rather than assumed: no credentials, no API keys, no
# internal paths (everything is relative to $ROOT). The only personal datum is
# the packager e-mail, which is already public in packaging/PKGBUILD line 1, in
# every commit author, and in the packager field of every Arch package already
# shipped — where it is meant to be, so users know who built it.
!/scripts/build-arch.sh

